hume-dialogues-4.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered yes, which keeps hume-dialogues-4.logic.r1, to: Suppose "possibly, pleasure alone, in its various degrees, is sufficient for exciting creatures to action and self-preservation" is true and "pleasure alone, in its various degrees, is sufficient for exciting creatures to action and self-preservation" is false. Is S2, "Now pleasure alone, in its various degrees, seems to human understanding sufficient for this purpose.", true then?
hume-dialogues-4.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: hume-dialogues-4.logic.r1 has "□(diminution_prompts → life_without_pain)"; hume-dialogues-4.logic.r1.2 and hume-dialogues-4.logic.r1.3 have "□(constant_enjoyment ∧ diminution_prompts → life_without_pain)"; they differ when only "necessarily, (if (all animals are constantly in a state of enjoyment and when urged by the necessities of nature, animals feel a diminution of pleasure instead of pain, which prompts them to seek what is necessary to their subsistence), then the business of life is carried on without any pain)" is true, which the second allows
hume-dialogues-4.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: hume-dialogues-4.logic.r1 takes for granted "necessarily, (if when urged by the necessities of nature, animals feel a diminution of pleasure instead of pain, which prompts them to seek what is necessary to their subsistence, then the business of life is carried on without any pain)"; hume-dialogues-4.logic.r1.2 and hume-dialogues-4.logic.r1.3 do not
hume-dialogues-4.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps hume-dialogues-4.logic.r1, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "necessarily, (if when urged by the necessities of nature, animals feel a diminution of pleasure instead of pain, which prompts them to seek what is necessary to their subsistence, then the business of life is carried on without any pain)"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
